From: WTA301 port <port@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: give IPQ5018/QCN6122 their own CE tables with
 smaller RX rings

Why this patch exists
---------------------
0950-wifi-ath11k-reduce-DP-ring-sizes-256M.patch only touches dp.h.  It was
believed to cover "the rings" of ath11k, but the Copy Engine rings live
somewhere else entirely: the per-pipe depths come from
ath11k_host_ce_config_*[] in ce.c, and that table was never touched.

Measured on this board after the dp.h change, with page_owner=on and the
allocation stacks aggregated by hand (433810 lines, 24482 allocated pages,
143 MiB total).  The per-stack ledger looked like this:

    24.0 MiB  shmem_get_folio            (tmpfs, reclaimable)
    22.2 MiB  __page_frag_alloc_align <- __netdev_alloc_skb <- CE path
    16.0 MiB  squashfs_readahead         (page cache, reclaimable)
    11.9 MiB  dma_alloc_attrs            (ath11k coherent rings)
     7.3 MiB  ath11k_dp_rxbufs_replenish
     6.5 MiB  ath11k_dp_tx_htt_monitor_mode_ring_config
     4.7 MiB  init_page_owner            (diagnostic build only)

The 22 MiB entry was still growing while the board idled: 21.8 -> 31.1 MiB
over six minutes of sampling, while Slab stayed flat at 23 MB and the
DP_LINK_DESC ring stayed flat at 10.0 MB.  So the leak is not slab, not the
DP rings that 0950 already shrank, and not the link descriptor ring.

IPQ5018 and QCN6122 both use ath11k_host_ce_config_qcn9074 (see core.c,
hw_rev ATH11K_HW_IPQ5018_HW10 and ATH11K_HW_QCN6122_HW10).  With ce_count=6
that table allocates receive buffers like this:

    CE1  dest_nentries = 512   src_sz_max = 2048   <- HTT data path
    CE5  dest_nentries = 512   src_sz_max = 2048   <- pktlog
    CE2  dest_nentries =   32                      <- WMI

i.e. 1024 receive buffers of 2 KiB per radio, 2048 for both radios, 4 MiB
nominal.  Measured usage is 22 MiB and climbing, so the page-fragment
allocator is amplifying it roughly 5.5x.  That is the well-known effect
described in openwrt/openwrt#24154: dev_alloc_skb() carves 2 KiB fragments
out of order-3 (32 KiB) pages, and a single still-referenced fragment pins
the whole page.  Sixteen fragments per page, freed out of order, means most
pages stay pinned far longer than a single buffer's lifetime.

The same upstream thread notes this is "bounded but oversized for the RAM"
rather than an unbounded leak, which is exactly what the numbers say: the
board does not run away to infinity, it sits 20-30 MiB lower than it should
and tips into the OOM killer whenever anything else (a sysupgrade write, an
firmware decompress) needs a few MiB.

What this patch changes
-----------------------
Instead of editing the shared qcn9074 table (which is also used by real
QCN9074 cards on other boards), add a dedicated ath11k_host_ce_config_ipq5018
and point both IPQ5018 and QCN6122 at it.  Only two depths move:

    CE1  (HTT data path)   512 -> 256
    CE5  (pktlog)          512 -> 128

CE2 (WMI, 32), CE3, CE4 and CE0 are copied verbatim.  CE4 keeps
src_nentries = 2048 with src_sz_max = 256: that is the TX side, 512 KiB of
256-byte descriptors, and it does not go through page_frag.

Why 256 is not a throughput risk on this board
----------------------------------------------
A 256-deep ring of 2 KiB buffers is 512 KiB of receive buffering per pipe.
The 5 GHz radio is an uplink relay whose station link measures -79..-90 dBm
and 13..26 Mbit/s, i.e. 1.6..3.3 MB/s: 512 KiB is 150-320 ms of buffering,
two orders of magnitude more than the bandwidth-delay product of that link.
Even a local 866 Mbit/s client blasting the AP at ~50 MB/s leaves ~10 ms,
which still covers NAPI jitter.  The ring was oversized for a 256 MiB board,
not sized for this hardware.

Note the firmware-side tables (ath11k_target_ce_config_wlan_ipq5018 in hw.c)
declare nentries = 32 for these pipes and are deliberately left alone: they
are the firmware's own bookkeeping, the host ring depth is what the host
posts, and it was already far above 32 before this patch.

RESULT: this patch does NOT fix the CE memory.  Measured on hardware.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Built, flashed and verified (ath11k.ko md5 39381f80362dece79a43df071867d41c,
symbol ath11k_host_ce_config_ipq5018 present, .rodata confirming CE1=0x100 and
CE5=0x80 while the untouched qcn9074 table still reads 0x200).  The CE
allocation did not move: 22.2 MiB before, 22.9 MiB after.  Kept in the tree
only as a record of the attempt -- it is harmless (smaller rings, no measured
throughput effect on a 13-26 Mbit/s relay) but it buys nothing.

The reason, from page_owner with all 8 stack frames captured (aggregating
3 frames is what misled the original analysis, because the third frame is
ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes, which only *frees*):

    10.7 MiB  <- ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes <- ath11k_ce_per_engine_service <- tasklet_action_common
     3.2 MiB  <- ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes <- ath11k_ce_per_engine_service <- 0xffffffc078f69fc0 <- tasklet
     0.3 MiB  <- ath11k_ce_rx_post_buf <- ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready

Only the 0.3 MiB is the boot-time pre-post driven by dest_nentries, i.e. the
only part ring depth controls.  The other 13.9 MiB is replenishment done one
packet at a time from the CE tasklet (ce.c:389 dev_alloc_skb).  Ring depth
cannot bound per-packet churn through a shared page_frag cache.

What would be needed is the per-ring page_frag_cache mechanism that
eisbaw/ath11k-page-frag-fix applies to the RXDMA rings -- but that series
explicitly leaves CE/HTC on the shared allocator, and CE is the worst
offender here (13.9 MiB vs ~6 MiB for RXDMA).  Porting it to ce.c's rx_post
path is driver surgery, not a constant change.

